''Metrosideros kermadecensis'' is categorised in the [[subgenus]] ''Metrosideros'' within the [[genus]] ''Metrosideros'',{{sfn|Bylsma|Clarkson|Efford|2014|p=5}} which consists of about 58 described species across Africa, Asia, Oceania and South America.{{sfn|Wright|Liddell|Lacap‐Bugler|Gillman|2021|p=1}}{{sfn|Pillon|Lucas|Johansen|Sakishima|2015|p=1}} The genus consists of two main subgenera: ''Metrosideros'' (trees) and ''Mearnsia ''(vines and shrubs).{{sfn|Bylsma|Clarkson|Efford|2014|p=5}}{{sfn|Wright|Liddell|Lacap‐Bugler|Gillman|2021|p=1}} There are twelve known species of ''[[Metrosideros]]'' in New Zealand; the subgenus ''Metrosideros'' comprises five tree species, ''[[Metrosideros bartlettii|M.&nbsp;bartlettii]]'', ''[[Pōhutukawa|M.&nbsp;excelsa]]'', ''M.&nbsp;kermadecensis'', ''[[Metrosideros robusta|M.&nbsp;robusta]]'', and ''[[Metrosideros umbellata|M.&nbsp;umbellata]]''; the other superseded subgenus, ''Mearnsia'', comprises six vine species and one shrub, ''[[Metrosideros albiflora|M.&nbsp;albiflora]]'', ''[[Metrosideros carminea|M.&nbsp;carminea]]'', ''[[Metrosideros colensoi|M.&nbsp;colensoi]]'', ''[[Metrosideros diffusa|M.&nbsp;diffusa]]'', ''[[Metrosideros fulgens|M.&nbsp;fulgens]]'', ''[[Metrosideros perforata|M.&nbsp;perforata]]'', and ''[[Metrosideros parkinsonii|M.&nbsp;parkinsonii]]''.{{sfn|Bylsma|Clarkson|Efford|2014|p=5}}{{sfn|Connor|Edgar|1987|p=20}}

In 2021, a cladistic analysis from ''[[Austral Ecology]]'' of the genus ''Metrosideros'', indicated a dispersal and radiation of the ''Metrosideros'' subgenus from New Zealand to [[Polynesia]], [[Lord Howe Island]] and the [[Kermadec Islands]]. Their analysis, using [[Ribosomal DNA|rDNA]] sequencing, suggested the [[Phylogenetics|phylogenetic]] relationships within this [[subclade]] suggested a separate dispersal route into East Polynesia, including "an apparent step" from the [[Marquesas Islands]] to [[Hawaii]]. ''Metrosideros kermadecensis'' and its related species are categorised in the [[clade]] (group) "Va".{{sfn|Wright|Liddell|Lacap‐Bugler|Gillman|2021|pp=1–5}}

===History===
''Metrosideros kermadecensis'' was first [[Species description|described]] by the British botanist [[Joseph Dalton Hooker]] in 1864, and was described as ''Metrosideros polymorpha''.{{sfn|Hooker|1864|p=73}} It was given its current [[binomial name]] in 1928 by the New Zealand botanist [[Walter Oliver]].{{Sfn|Oliver|1928|pp=422–423}} His rationale for moving the species into a separate taxon was that it had smaller flowers, and its leaves were more oval-shaped and glabrous than other species he was comparing it to, such as ''M. collina'', ''M. excelsa'', and ''M. polymorpha''.{{Sfn|Oliver|1928|pp=422–423}}

===Etymology===
The [[etymology]] (word origin) of ''M. kermadecensis''{{'}}s genus ''Metrosideros'' translates to English from [[Greek language|Greek]] as 'iron-heart': the word {{lang|el|metra}} means 'core' or 'heart', and {{lang|el|sideron}} means 'iron',{{Sfn|De Lange|2024}}{{sfn|Bylsma|Clarkson|Efford|2014|p=4}} alluding to the timber's iron-like strength.{{Sfn|Laing|1906|p=238}} The [[specific epithet]] (second part of the scientific name), ''kermadecensis'', is a [[Latinisation of names|Latinisation]] after the species' native distribution area, the Kermadec Islands.{{Sfn|De Lange|2024}} The species is [[common name|commonly known]] as the Kermadec pōhutukawa.{{Sfn|De Lange|2025}}{{sfn|Ho|Baskarathevan|Griffin|Quinn|2019|p=1}}

==Ecology==
''Metrosideros kermadecensis'' plays host to the endemic beetle ''[[Oemona hirta]]''.{{Sfn|Sopow|Bain|2017|p=12}} It also plays host to the insects ''[[Uraba lugens]]'' and ''[[Ornithodoros|Ornithodoros capensis]]''.{{sfn|Withers|Potter|Berndt|Forgie|2011|p=8}}{{sfn|Ramsay|1968|p=1}} The native moss and liverwort species, ''Syrrhopodon armatus'' and ''Cheilolejeunea trifaria'', respectively,  have been observed growing on ''M. kermadecensis''.{{sfn|Beever|Fife|West|1996|p=3}}{{sfn|Renner|De Lange|2011|p=4}} ''[[Peniophora sacrata]]'', a species of fungus, has also been observed growing on ''M. kermadecensis''.{{sfn|Taylor|Guy|1981|p=2}} ''M. kermadecensis'' has been recorded to be browsed by introduced goats, mostly consuming the more accessible parts of the tree, such as the [[seedling]]s, [[Shoot (botany)|shoots]], and trunks, consisting of  about 32 percent of their diet.{{sfn|Parkes|1984|p=3}}

==Distribution==
''Metrosideros kermadecensis'' is [[endemic]] to the [[Kermadec Islands]], where it is found on [[Raoul Island]], and the North and South Meyer Islands.{{Sfn|De Lange|2024}} ''M. kermadecensis'' is also found on the Herald Islets, which includes the Dayrell, Napier, and Nugent islets, which are part of the Kermadec Island chain.{{Sfn|De Lange|2024}} They are found {{cvt|1000|km|mi nmi}} northeast from mainland New Zealand.{{Sfn|Bercusson|Torrence|1998|p=43}} The species was supposedly  present on [[Macauley Island]], but the New Zealand botanist [[Peter de Lange (botanist)|Peter de Lange]], disputes this, as there is no documentation.{{Sfn|De Lange|2024}}

===Habitat===
It occurs from almost sea level to {{convert|516|m|abbr=on|sigfig=1}} above sea level, ranging from the coastline to the highest altitudes of the islands. ''M. kermadecensis'' is commonly associated with ''[[Alsophila kermadecensis]]'' and ''[[Rhopalostylis baueri]]'' in the forests. ''M. kermadecensis'' is the dominant tree species on Raoul Island, the largest island in the Kermadec Island chain.{{Sfn|De Lange|2024}}{{Sfn|De Lange|2025}}

==Conservation==
''Metrosideros kermadecensis''{{'}}s [[conservation status]] was assessed by the [[IUCN Red List]] in 2023 as "Vulnerable", and its population trend was evaluated as "Decreasing".{{Sfn|De Lange|2025}} In 2017, it was reported that [[myrtle rust]], an invasive species of fungus, was discovered growing on ''M. kermadecensis''.{{sfn|Ho|Baskarathevan|Griffin|Quinn|2019|p=1}} It commonly affects Myrtaceae species, and specimens of ''M. kermadecensis'' have died after being infected by the fungus.{{Sfn|De Lange|2025}}{{sfn|Toome-Heller|Ho|Ganley|Elliott|2020|p=1}}

